				<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-79f5322 elementor-widget elementor-widget-theme-post-excerpt" data-id="79f5322" data-element_type="widget" data-e-type="widget" data-widget_type="theme-post-excerpt.default">
					The Allied Powers of World War II formed one of the most important alliances in history, and their victory helped shape the modern world that emerged after the end of the Second World War.				</div>
				<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-294611b elementor-widget elementor-widget-theme-post-content" data-id="294611b" data-element_type="widget" data-e-type="widget" data-widget_type="theme-post-content.default">
					
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The Allied Powers of <a href="https://historycrunch.com/world-war-ii/" data-type="page" data-id="8207">World War II</a> were <a href="https://historycrunch.com/britain-in-world-war-ii/" data-type="post" data-id="7160">Britain</a>, the <a href="https://historycrunch.com/soviet-union-in-world-war-ii/" data-type="post" data-id="7168">Soviet Union</a>, the <a href="https://historycrunch.com/united-states-in-world-war-ii/" data-type="post" data-id="7178">United States</a> and China. Together, these four nations are often called the &#8216;Big Four&#8217; of the Allied Powers. With that said, <a href="https://historycrunch.com/france-in-world-war-ii/" data-type="post" data-id="7154">Free France</a>, led by <a href="https://historycrunch.com/charles-de-gaulle/" data-type="post" data-id="6823">Charles de Gaulle</a>, also became an important part of the Allied cause after <a href="https://historycrunch.com/battle-of-france-in-world-war-ii/" data-type="post" data-id="3498">France fell to Nazi Germany</a> in 1940. Together, these nations formed the coalition that ultimately defeated the <a href="https://historycrunch.com/axis-powers-of-world-war-ii/" data-type="post" data-id="6672">Axis Powers of World War II</a>, which included <a href="https://historycrunch.com/nazi-germany-in-world-war-ii/" data-type="post" data-id="7202">Nazi Germany</a>, <a href="https://historycrunch.com/italy-in-world-war-ii/" data-type="post" data-id="7186">fascist Italy</a> and <a href="https://historycrunch.com/imperial-japan-in-world-war-ii/" data-type="post" data-id="7193">Imperial Japan</a>.</p><!--ftc-embed-start-->

<div id="ftcasd47p" class="ftc-embed ftc-lean" data-ftcv="773" style="max-width:920px;margin:0 auto">
<div class="ftc-card-scope" data-type="bullbio" data-category="Bulletin Boards" data-topic="World War II" data-subtopic="Allied Leaders" data-title="Charles de Gaulle" data-source="Imperial War Museums"><div class="card img-none cc-vig box-lift bs-raised bsh-rounded acc-glow fx-lift nb-spiral nb-tape nb-torn nb-doodle nb-hlhand nb-dogear nb-overshoot fx-sheen expr-round lc-title fbs-gloss fwm" style="--cc-bg:#faf0ef;--hl:#ffd54f;--ul:#36e0c8;--accent:#b3271b;--acc-glow:rgba(179,39,27,0.3);--acc-glow2:rgba(179,39,27,0.186);--acc-glow-blur:26px;--flip-h:100px;--lead-scale:1;--rp-arrow-size:30px;--rp-arrow-gap:12px;--tp-arrow-size:34px;--kt-termw:150px;--ps-gap:8px;--ven-size:100%;--def-pad:7px;--mtl-yr-w:98px;--exp-fill:rgba(179,39,27,0.1);--g3d:1.3;--flip-bg:#3a3a3a;--flip-light:#e3e3e3;--flip-cols:2;--flip-icon-size:42px;--flip-fsize:15px;--flip-weight:600;--tex-op:0.19;--vs-size:40px;--vs-pad:10px;--bar-h:18px;--ce-arrow:60px;--ce-arrow-pad:8px;--img-fr:0.72fr;--imgcolw:40%;--tex-url:url('https://historycrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/Concrete-2.png');--tex-tf:scaleX(-1) scaleY(-1);--vig-img-blur:40px;--vig-img-op:0.45;--vig-cc-blur:20px;--vig-cc-op:0.4;--fade-dur:4s;box-shadow:3px 6px 22px rgba(0,0,0,0.24),0 1px 4px rgba(0,0,0,0.05)"><div class="ch"><div class="ch-bar"><div class="hl"><span class="ctt">World War II</span><span class="cty" style="color:#b3271b">Bulletin Board Bio</span><span class="ix-badge">Interactive</span></div><a class="logo-link" href="https://factsthatcrunch.com" target="_blank" style="transform:translate(0px,5px);" rel="noopener"><img decoding="async" style="height:36px" src="https://factsthatcrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/facts-that-crunch-logo.png" alt="Facts that Crunch" onerror="this.style.display='none';this.nextElementSibling.style.display='flex'"><span class="logo-fb"><span class="lb"><span>Facts</span></span><span class="lt">that</span><span class="lb"><span>Crunch</span></span></span></a></div><div class="ch-line"><div class="ch-line-fill" style="background:#b3271b"></div></div></div><div class="cb"><div class="cc" style="background:#9a6a38"><div class="ic-eb ic-bb-board" data-itype="board" style="background-image:url(https://historycrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/Cork-4-scaled.png);background-size:cover;background-position:center;--eb-scrim:0.70"><div class="ic-bb-row"><div class="ic-bb-leftcol"><div class="ic-bb-left"><span class="ic-mb-tack"></span><img class="ic-bb-img" src="https://historycrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Charles-de-Gaulle-1024x576.jpg" alt="Charles de Gaulle in 1942. (Colorized by historycrunch.com)" loading="lazy" decoding="async" referrerpolicy="no-referrer" onerror="this.style.display=&#39;none&#39;"><div class="ic-bb-cardtx"><span class="ic-bb-name">Charles de Gaulle</span></div></div><div class="ic-bb-tab"><span class="ic-mb-tack"></span><span class="ic-bb-tab-hd">At a Glance</span><div class="ic-bb-tab-row"><span class="ic-bb-tab-k">Born</span><span class="ic-bb-tab-v">November 22nd, 1890, in Lille, France</span></div><div class="ic-bb-tab-row"><span class="ic-bb-tab-k">Died</span><span class="ic-bb-tab-v">November 9th, 1970, in Colombey-les-Deux-Eglises, France</span></div><div class="ic-bb-tab-row"><span class="ic-bb-tab-k">Role</span><span class="ic-bb-tab-v">French Army General and Leader of Free France</span></div></div></div><div class="ic-bb-right"><div class="ic-bb-hint">Tap a heading to open the note.</div><div class="ic-eb-item ic-bb-fact" data-eb="0" style="transform:rotate(0deg)"><span class="ic-eb-pin"></span><div class="ic-mb-lab">Early Military Career</div><div class="ic-eb-det">De Gaulle was wounded three times and taken prisoner during World War I, spending nearly three years as a German prisoner of war.</div></div><div class="ic-eb-item ic-bb-fact" data-eb="1" style="transform:rotate(-1deg)"><span class="ic-eb-pin"></span><div class="ic-mb-lab">June 18th Appeal</div><div class="ic-eb-det">On June 18th, 1940, de Gaulle broadcast a radio address from London urging French people to keep fighting, refusing to accept France&#39;s surrender to Germany.</div></div><div class="ic-eb-item ic-bb-fact" data-eb="2" style="transform:rotate(3deg)"><span class="ic-eb-pin"></span><div class="ic-mb-lab">Free France</div><div class="ic-eb-det">De Gaulle led the Free French Forces from London, organizing French troops and resistance fighters who continued the war effort after France fell.</div></div><div class="ic-eb-item ic-bb-fact" data-eb="3" style="transform:rotate(-2deg)"><span class="ic-eb-pin"></span><div class="ic-mb-lab">Relations with the Allies</div><div class="ic-eb-det">De Gaulle had frequent disagreements with both Winston Churchill and Franklin Roosevelt, who were slow to recognize him as the official leader of France.</div></div><div class="ic-eb-item ic-bb-fact" data-eb="4" style="transform:rotate(-2deg)"><span class="ic-eb-pin"></span><div class="ic-mb-lab">Liberation of Paris</div><div class="ic-eb-det">On August 25th, 1944, German forces in Paris surrendered, and de Gaulle led a march down the Champs-Elysees the following day to mark the city&#39;s liberation.</div></div><div class="ic-eb-item ic-bb-fact" data-eb="5" style="transform:rotate(-2deg)"><span class="ic-eb-pin"></span><div class="ic-mb-lab">Postwar Leadership</div><div class="ic-eb-det">De Gaulle served as head of the French provisional government after the war and later founded the Fifth Republic, becoming France&#39;s president in January 1959.</div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="cf" style="background:#fff;border-top:1px solid #f0f0f0"><div class="cf-top"><span class="cf-src" style="color:#999" title="Source: Imperial War Museums">Source: Imperial War Museums</span><span class="cf-copy" style="color:#999">© factsthatcrunch.com</span><a class="cf-more" style="color:#b3271b" href="https://factsthatcrunch.com/world-war-ii-facts/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">More World War II Facts →</a></div></div></div></div>
</div>

<!--ftc-embed-end-->




<div style="height:50px" aria-hidden="true" class="wp-block-spacer"></div>



<h2 id="what-was-world-war-ii" class="wp-block-heading">WHAT WAS WORLD WAR II?</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><a href="https://historycrunch.com/world-war-ii-overview/" data-type="post" data-id="7312">World War II</a> lasted from 1939 to 1945 and became the largest and deadliest war in all of human history. World War II began on September 1st, 1939 when <a href="https://historycrunch.com/nazi-germany/" data-type="page" data-id="9473">Nazi Germany</a>, under the leadership of <a href="https://historycrunch.com/adolf-hitler/" data-type="post" data-id="2935">Adolf Hitler</a>, <a href="https://historycrunch.com/nazi-germany-invasion-of-poland/" data-type="post" data-id="3410">invaded the country of Poland</a>. The war unfolded as a series of major battles that spanned both the <a href="https://historycrunch.com/european-theater-of-world-war-ii/" data-type="post" data-id="7219">European</a> and <a href="https://historycrunch.com/pacific-theater-of-world-war-ii/" data-type="post" data-id="7223">Pacific</a> Theaters of war. In Europe, the early years of World War II were dominated by battles, such as: <a href="https://historycrunch.com/battle-of-france-in-world-war-ii/" data-type="post" data-id="3498">Battle of France</a>, <a href="https://historycrunch.com/battle-of-britain-in-world-war-ii/" data-type="post" data-id="3506">Battle of Britain</a>, <a href="https://historycrunch.com/operation-barbarossa-in-world-war-ii/" data-type="post" data-id="3519">Operation Barbarossa</a> and the <a href="https://historycrunch.com/battle-of-stalingrad-in-world-war-ii/" data-type="post" data-id="3531">Battle of Stalingrad</a>. The later years saw the Allies liberate Europe through the <a href="https://historycrunch.com/allied-invasion-of-italy-in-world-war-ii/" data-type="post" data-id="3591">invasion of Sicily</a> and the <a href="https://historycrunch.com/normandy-invasion-d-day-of-world-war-ii/" data-type="post" data-id="3558">Normandy Landings</a> on D-Day. In the Pacific, the United States faced off against Imperial Japan in major conflicts, such as the <a href="https://historycrunch.com/battle-of-midway-in-world-war-ii/" data-type="post" data-id="3576">Battle of Midway</a> and the <a href="https://historycrunch.com/battle-of-iwo-jima-in-world-war-ii/" data-type="post" data-id="3579">Battle of Iwo Jima</a>.</p><!--ftc-embed-start-->

<div id="ftckhbrck" class="ftc-embed ftc-lean" data-ftcv="773" style="max-width:920px;margin:0 auto">
<div class="ftc-card-scope" data-type="date" data-category="Information" data-topic="World War II" data-subtopic="How Did World War II Start?" data-title="" data-source="Smithsonian Institution"><div class="card cc-vig box-lift bs-raised bsh-rounded fx-lift nb-spiral nb-tape nb-torn nb-doodle nb-hlhand nb-dogear nb-overshoot fx-sheen expr-round lc-title fbs-gloss fwm" style="--cc-bg:#eff8f5;--hl:#ffd54f;--ul:#36e0c8;--accent:#1D9E75;--acc-glow:rgba(29,158,117,0.3);--acc-glow2:rgba(29,158,117,0.186);--acc-glow-blur:26px;--flip-h:100px;--lead-scale:1;--rp-arrow-size:30px;--rp-arrow-gap:12px;--tp-arrow-size:34px;--kt-termw:150px;--ps-gap:8px;--ven-size:100%;--def-pad:7px;--mtl-yr-w:98px;--exp-fill:rgba(29,158,117,0.1);--g3d:1.3;--flip-bg:#3a3a3a;--flip-light:#e3e3e3;--flip-cols:2;--flip-icon-size:42px;--flip-fsize:15px;--flip-weight:600;--tex-op:0.22;--vs-size:40px;--vs-pad:10px;--bar-h:18px;--ce-arrow:60px;--ce-arrow-pad:8px;--img-fr:0.72fr;--imgcolw:40%;--tex-url:url('https://historycrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/Paper-2.png');--tex-tf:scaleX(-1) scaleY(-1);--vig-img-blur:40px;--vig-img-op:0.45;--vig-cc-blur:20px;--vig-cc-op:0.4;--fade-dur:4s;box-shadow:3px 6px 22px rgba(0,0,0,0.24),0 1px 4px rgba(0,0,0,0.05)"><div class="ch"><div class="ch-bar"><div class="hl"><span class="ctt">World War II</span><span class="cty" style="color:#1D9E75">Date</span></div><a class="logo-link" href="https://factsthatcrunch.com" target="_blank" style="transform:translate(0px,5px);" rel="noopener"><img decoding="async" style="height:36px" src="https://factsthatcrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/facts-that-crunch-logo.png" alt="Facts that Crunch" onerror="this.style.display='none';this.nextElementSibling.style.display='flex'"><span class="logo-fb"><span class="lb"><span>Facts</span></span><span class="lt">that</span><span class="lb"><span>Crunch</span></span></span></a></div><div class="ch-line"><div class="ch-line-fill" style="background:#1D9E75"></div></div></div><div class="cb"><div class="dh" style="background:#f5f4f0"><div class="dh-top"><div class="dd" style="color:#1D9E75">1st</div><div class="ddi" style="background:#1D9E75"></div><div class="dmy" style="color:#1D9E75">September 1939</div></div><div class="dh-bottom"><p class="dev" style="color:#111">Germany Invades Poland, Starting World War II</p><p class="dct" style="color:#666">In the early morning of September 1st, 1939, German forces attacked Poland from three directions using more than 1.5 million soldiers, over 2,000 tanks, and nearly 900 bombers. The German battleship Schleswig-Holstein opened fire on the small Polish military depot at Westerplatte, near the port of Danzig, at around 4:43 a.m., while Luftwaffe bombers struck the Polish town of Wielun at roughly the same time with no military targets present. Britain and France declared war on Germany two days later, on September 3rd, 1939, turning the invasion into a global conflict.</p></div></div><div class="ci fx-vig tr-fade" style="background:#000000"><img loading="lazy" loading="lazy" src="https://historycrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Nazi-Germany-Invasion-of-Poland-1024x576.jpg" alt="Adolf Hitler watching German soldiers march into Poland in September of 1939. (Colorized by historycrunch.com)" width="1280" height="720" loading="lazy" decoding="async" referrerpolicy="no-referrer" onerror="this.style.display='none';this.parentElement.querySelector('.ci-ph').style.display='flex'"><div class="ci-scrim ci-scrim-bottom" style="--scrim-op:0.55;--scrim-h:45%"></div><div class="ci-cap ci-cap-bottom ci-cap-small" style="text-align:center;transform:translate(0px,0px)">Adolf Hitler watching German soldiers march into Poland in September of 1939. (Colorized by historycrunch.com)</div><div class="ci-ph" style="display:none"><svg width="28" height="28" viewBox="0 0 28 28" fill="none" style="opacity:0.3"><rect x="2" y="5" width="24" height="18" rx="2" stroke="white" stroke-width="2" fill="none"/><path d="M2 18L9 12L14 17L20 10L26 18" stroke="white" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg><span>Image failed to load</span></div></div></div><div class="cf" style="background:#f5f4f0;border-top:1px solid #e8e6e0"><div class="cf-top"><span class="cf-src" style="color:#888" title="Source: Smithsonian Institution">Source: Smithsonian Institution</span><span class="cf-copy" style="color:#888">© factsthatcrunch.com</span><a class="cf-more" style="color:#1D9E75" href="https://factsthatcrunch.com/world-war-ii-facts/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">More World War II Facts →</a></div></div></div></div>
</div>

<!--ftc-embed-end-->




<div style="height:50px" aria-hidden="true" class="wp-block-spacer"></div>



<h2 id="allied-powers-of-world-war-ii-history-and-member-nations" class="wp-block-heading">ALLIED POWERS OF WORLD WAR II &#8211; HISTORY AND MEMBER NATIONS</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The Allied Powers were a military and political coalition of countries that opposed the Axis Powers during World War II, and their shared purpose was to defeat the aggressive expansion of <a href="https://historycrunch.com/nazi-germany-overview/" data-type="post" data-id="7319">Nazi Germany</a>, Italy and Imperial Japan. However, the Allies were not united because they all believed the same things or shared a united vision for the world. In fact, the alliance brought together democracies such as Britain and the United States, the communist Soviet Union under <a href="https://historycrunch.com/joseph-stalin/" data-type="post" data-id="2970">Joseph Stalin</a>, and Nationalist China under Chiang Kai-shek. These countries often disagreed with one another and sometimes deeply distrusted one another, but they understood that none of them could defeat the Axis Powers alone.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The Allied coalition also included many other nations, colonies, and governments-in-exile that had been forced from their homelands by Axis military aggression. For example, after France was defeated by Nazi Germany during the <a href="https://historycrunch.com/battle-of-france-in-world-war-ii/" data-type="post" data-id="3498">Battle of France</a> in June of 1940, <a href="https://historycrunch.com/charles-de-gaulle/" data-type="post" data-id="6823">Charles de Gaulle</a> called on the French people to continue resisting Nazi rule. His efforts helped create the Free French movement.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">As mentioned above the &#8216;Big Four&#8217; nations of the Allied Powers were Britain, the Soviet Union, the United States and China. Britain, under Prime Minister <a href="https://historycrunch.com/winston-churchill/" data-type="post" data-id="6442">Winston Churchill</a>, remained in the war even after the fall of France in 1940 and became the main base of resistance against Nazi Germany in Western Europe. The Soviet Union, led by Joseph Stalin, carried much of the ground fighting against Nazi Germany in eastern Europe after <a href="https://historycrunch.com/adolf-hitler/" data-type="post" data-id="2935">Adolf Hitler</a> launched <a href="https://historycrunch.com/operation-barbarossa-in-world-war-ii/" data-type="post" data-id="3519">Operation Barbarossa</a> on June 22nd, 1941. The United States, led first by <a href="https://historycrunch.com/franklin-d-roosevelt/" data-type="post" data-id="2046">Franklin D. Roosevelt</a> and later by <a href="https://historycrunch.com/harry-truman/" data-type="post" data-id="3218">Harry S. Truman</a>, provided enormous industrial power, military strength, weapons, ships and financial support. The United States officially joined the war effort of World War II on the side of the Allies, following the Surprise attack that Japan carried out against Pearl Harbor. At the same time, China, under Chiang Kai-shek, continued resisting Japan and tied down large numbers of Japanese troops in Asia.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The Allied war effort was shaped not only by battles but also by important meetings among Allied leaders, because strategy had to be coordinated across multiple continents and fronts. One of the most important of these meetings was the Tehran Conference, which took place from November 28th to December 1st, 1943. This was the first major face-to-face meeting of Churchill, Roosevelt, and Stalin, and during the conference they discussed how to defeat Germany and agreed on the need for a major Allied invasion of western Europe.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Another crucial meeting was the <a href="https://historycrunch.com/yalta-conference/" data-type="post" data-id="3133">Yalta Conference</a>, which was held from February 4th to February 11th, 1945, when Allied victory in Europe was approaching. At Yalta, the &#8216;Big Three&#8217; (Churchill, Roosevelt, and Stalin) discussed the final defeat of Nazi Germany, the occupation of German territory, the future of Poland, and the creation of a new international organization that would later become the <a href="https://historycrunch.com/united-nations/" data-type="post" data-id="2997">United Nations</a>. Last was the <a href="https://historycrunch.com/potsdam-conference/" data-type="post" data-id="3128">Potsdam Conference</a>, which was held from July 17th to August 2nd, 1945.</p><!--ftc-embed-start-->

<div id="ftchhvynv" class="ftc-embed ftc-lean" data-ftcv="773" style="max-width:920px;margin:0 auto">
<div class="ftc-card-scope" data-type="flip" data-category="Explore" data-topic="World War II" data-subtopic="France in World War II" data-title="" data-source="Smithsonian Institution"><div class="card tex-parchment img-none cc-vig box-lift bs-raised bsh-rounded acc-glow fx-lift nb-spiral nb-tape nb-torn nb-doodle nb-hlhand nb-dogear nb-overshoot fx-sheen expr-round lc-title fbs-gloss fwm" style="--cc-bg:#f9f6ef;--hl:#ffd54f;--ul:#36e0c8;--accent:#B0851E;--acc-glow:rgba(176,133,30,0.3);--acc-glow2:rgba(176,133,30,0.186);--acc-glow-blur:26px;--flip-h:100px;--lead-scale:1;--rp-arrow-size:30px;--rp-arrow-gap:12px;--tp-arrow-size:34px;--kt-termw:150px;--ps-gap:8px;--ven-size:100%;--def-pad:7px;--mtl-yr-w:98px;--exp-fill:rgba(176,133,30,0.1);--g3d:1.3;--flip-bg:#3a3a3a;--flip-light:#e3e3e3;--flip-cols:2;--flip-icon-size:42px;--flip-fsize:15px;--flip-weight:600;--tex-op:0.19;--vs-size:40px;--vs-pad:10px;--bar-h:18px;--ce-arrow:60px;--ce-arrow-pad:8px;--img-fr:0.72fr;--imgcolw:40%;--tex-url:url('https://historycrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/Paper-5.png');--tex-tf:rotate(180deg);--vig-img-blur:40px;--vig-img-op:0.45;--vig-cc-blur:20px;--vig-cc-op:0.4;--fade-dur:4s;box-shadow:3px 6px 22px rgba(0,0,0,0.24),0 1px 4px rgba(0,0,0,0.05)"><div class="ch"><div class="ch-bar"><div class="hl"><span class="ctt">World War II</span><span class="cty" style="color:#B0851E">Flip Notebook</span><span class="ix-badge">Interactive</span></div><a class="logo-link" href="https://factsthatcrunch.com" target="_blank" style="transform:translate(0px,5px);" rel="noopener"><img decoding="async" style="height:36px" src="https://factsthatcrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/facts-that-crunch-logo.png" alt="Facts that Crunch" onerror="this.style.display='none';this.nextElementSibling.style.display='flex'"><span class="logo-fb"><span class="lb"><span>Facts</span></span><span class="lt">that</span><span class="lb"><span>Crunch</span></span></span></a></div><div class="ch-line"><div class="ch-line-fill" style="background:#B0851E"></div></div></div><div class="cb"><div class="cc" style="background:var(--cc-bg,#fff)"><p class="ic-intro">Which governments kept fighting after Axis forces seized their countries, and how did <span class="hl-phrase">governments-in-exile</span> operate during World War II?</p><div class="ic-flipgrid" data-itype="flip"><button type="button" class="ic-flip"><span class="ic-flip-inner"><span class="ic-flip-face ic-flip-front"><span class="ic-flip-icon"><svg class="ph-ic ph-vec" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 256 256" fill="currentColor"><path d="M42.76,50A8,8,0,0,0,40,56V224a8,8,0,0,0,16,0V179.77c26.79-21.16,49.87-9.75,76.45,3.41,16.4,8.11,34.06,16.85,53,16.85,13.93,0,28.54-4.75,43.82-18a8,8,0,0,0,2.76-6V56A8,8,0,0,0,218.76,50c-28,24.23-51.72,12.49-79.21-1.12C111.07,34.76,78.78,18.79,42.76,50ZM216,172.25c-26.79,21.16-49.87,9.74-76.45-3.41-25-12.35-52.81-26.13-83.55-8.4V59.79c26.79-21.16,49.87-9.75,76.45,3.4,25,12.35,52.82,26.13,83.55,8.4Z"/></svg></span><span class="ic-flip-lbl">Polish Government-in-Exile</span></span><span class="ic-flip-face ic-flip-back"><span class="ic-flip-bhead"><span class="ic-flip-bicon"><svg class="ph-ic ph-vec" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 256 256" fill="currentColor"><path d="M42.76,50A8,8,0,0,0,40,56V224a8,8,0,0,0,16,0V179.77c26.79-21.16,49.87-9.75,76.45,3.41,16.4,8.11,34.06,16.85,53,16.85,13.93,0,28.54-4.75,43.82-18a8,8,0,0,0,2.76-6V56A8,8,0,0,0,218.76,50c-28,24.23-51.72,12.49-79.21-1.12C111.07,34.76,78.78,18.79,42.76,50ZM216,172.25c-26.79,21.16-49.87,9.74-76.45-3.41-25-12.35-52.81-26.13-83.55-8.4V59.79c26.79-21.16,49.87-9.75,76.45,3.4,25,12.35,52.82,26.13,83.55,8.4Z"/></svg></span><span class="ic-flip-btitle">Polish Government-in-Exile</span></span><span class="ic-flip-bt">Poland&#39;s government fled to Romania after Germany invaded in September 1939, then moved to Paris, and finally settled in London. It directed the Polish Armed Forces in the West and ran a courier network into occupied Poland, including the famous courier Jan Karski.</span></span></span></button><button type="button" class="ic-flip"><span class="ic-flip-inner"><span class="ic-flip-face ic-flip-front"><span class="ic-flip-icon"><svg class="ph-ic ph-vec" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 512 512" fill="currentColor"><path d="M476.72 51.375c-5.28 30.185-124.992 107.9-82.47 16.875-80.216 45.38-107.557 23.42-78.53-5.656-54.825 2.8-62.753 88.173-55.345 112.406l17.438 19.125c-2.14-31.218.404-48.445 19.5-71.47 13.764 20.614 18.495 33.702 52.062 6.97-9.393 53.52 54.61 18.747 88.75 10.938-15.248 14.048-35.153 32.723-38.875 55.468-1.24 7.587 6.208 17.925 14.125 25.626-9.443 2.236-41.474 8.91-38.563 26.22 2.912 17.31 12.14 11.885 3.5 15.28-12.403 2.766-21.156 5.58-39.593-2.187l18.874 20.717c28.39 14.79 73.904 7.306 83.594-14.875-14.778-1.22-27.125-4.674-33-11.53 44.022-8.34 66.764-39.243 85.78-75.032-33.638 18.95-42.158 17.784-56 16.313 35.514-14.365 46.876-108.943 38.75-145.188zM246.874 186.063l-56.78 70.125 79.186 86.906 75.095-50-97.5-107.03zm-62.344 90.125L21.657 467.625l21.438 23.53 205.75-144.374-64.313-70.592z"/></svg></span><span class="ic-flip-lbl">French National Committee</span></span><span class="ic-flip-face ic-flip-back"><span class="ic-flip-bhead"><span class="ic-flip-bicon"><svg class="ph-ic ph-vec" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 512 512" fill="currentColor"><path d="M476.72 51.375c-5.28 30.185-124.992 107.9-82.47 16.875-80.216 45.38-107.557 23.42-78.53-5.656-54.825 2.8-62.753 88.173-55.345 112.406l17.438 19.125c-2.14-31.218.404-48.445 19.5-71.47 13.764 20.614 18.495 33.702 52.062 6.97-9.393 53.52 54.61 18.747 88.75 10.938-15.248 14.048-35.153 32.723-38.875 55.468-1.24 7.587 6.208 17.925 14.125 25.626-9.443 2.236-41.474 8.91-38.563 26.22 2.912 17.31 12.14 11.885 3.5 15.28-12.403 2.766-21.156 5.58-39.593-2.187l18.874 20.717c28.39 14.79 73.904 7.306 83.594-14.875-14.778-1.22-27.125-4.674-33-11.53 44.022-8.34 66.764-39.243 85.78-75.032-33.638 18.95-42.158 17.784-56 16.313 35.514-14.365 46.876-108.943 38.75-145.188zM246.874 186.063l-56.78 70.125 79.186 86.906 75.095-50-97.5-107.03zm-62.344 90.125L21.657 467.625l21.438 23.53 205.75-144.374-64.313-70.592z"/></svg></span><span class="ic-flip-btitle">French National Committee</span></span><span class="ic-flip-bt">After France fell in June 1940, Charles de Gaulle established the Free French movement in London. It was recognized by Britain as the legitimate French authority and coordinated resistance networks inside occupied France.</span></span></span></button><button type="button" class="ic-flip"><span class="ic-flip-inner"><span class="ic-flip-face ic-flip-front"><span class="ic-flip-icon"><svg class="ph-ic ph-vec" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 256 256" fill="currentColor"><path d="M216,136a8,8,0,0,0-8,8c0,24.69-13.77,29.64-38.1,36.28-11.36,3.1-24.12,6.6-33.9,14.34V128h32a8,8,0,0,0,0-16H136V87a32,32,0,1,0-16,0v25H88a8,8,0,0,0,0,16h32v66.62c-9.78-7.74-22.54-11.24-33.9-14.34C61.77,173.64,48,168.69,48,144a8,8,0,0,0-16,0c0,38.11,27.67,45.66,49.9,51.72C106.23,202.36,120,207.31,120,232a8,8,0,0,0,16,0c0-24.69,13.77-29.64,38.1-36.28C196.33,189.66,224,182.11,224,144A8,8,0,0,0,216,136ZM112,56a16,16,0,1,1,16,16A16,16,0,0,1,112,56Z"/></svg></span><span class="ic-flip-lbl">Norwegian Government-in-Exile</span></span><span class="ic-flip-face ic-flip-back"><span class="ic-flip-bhead"><span class="ic-flip-bicon"><svg class="ph-ic ph-vec" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 256 256" fill="currentColor"><path d="M216,136a8,8,0,0,0-8,8c0,24.69-13.77,29.64-38.1,36.28-11.36,3.1-24.12,6.6-33.9,14.34V128h32a8,8,0,0,0,0-16H136V87a32,32,0,1,0-16,0v25H88a8,8,0,0,0,0,16h32v66.62c-9.78-7.74-22.54-11.24-33.9-14.34C61.77,173.64,48,168.69,48,144a8,8,0,0,0-16,0c0,38.11,27.67,45.66,49.9,51.72C106.23,202.36,120,207.31,120,232a8,8,0,0,0,16,0c0-24.69,13.77-29.64,38.1-36.28C196.33,189.66,224,182.11,224,144A8,8,0,0,0,216,136ZM112,56a16,16,0,1,1,16,16A16,16,0,0,1,112,56Z"/></svg></span><span class="ic-flip-btitle">Norwegian Government-in-Exile</span></span><span class="ic-flip-bt">King Haakon VII and his cabinet escaped to Britain in June 1940 after Germany completed its occupation of Norway. The government controlled Norway&#39;s large merchant fleet from London, which carried vital Allied supplies across the Atlantic.</span></span></span></button><button type="button" class="ic-flip"><span class="ic-flip-inner"><span class="ic-flip-face ic-flip-front"><span class="ic-flip-icon"><svg class="ph-ic ph-vec" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 256 256" fill="currentColor"><path d="M248,80a28,28,0,1,0-51.12,15.77l-26.79,33L146,73.4a28,28,0,1,0-36.06,0L85.91,128.74l-26.79-33a28,28,0,1,0-26.6,12L47,194.63A16,16,0,0,0,62.78,208H193.22A16,16,0,0,0,209,194.63l14.47-86.85A28,28,0,0,0,248,80ZM128,40a12,12,0,1,1-12,12A12,12,0,0,1,128,40ZM24,80A12,12,0,1,1,36,92,12,12,0,0,1,24,80ZM193.22,192H62.78L48.86,108.52,81.79,149A8,8,0,0,0,88,152a7.83,7.83,0,0,0,1.08-.07,8,8,0,0,0,6.26-4.74l29.3-67.4a27,27,0,0,0,6.72,0l29.3,67.4a8,8,0,0,0,6.26,4.74A7.83,7.83,0,0,0,168,152a8,8,0,0,0,6.21-3l32.93-40.52ZM220,92a12,12,0,1,1,12-12A12,12,0,0,1,220,92Z"/></svg></span><span class="ic-flip-lbl">Dutch Government-in-Exile</span></span><span class="ic-flip-face ic-flip-back"><span class="ic-flip-bhead"><span class="ic-flip-bicon"><svg class="ph-ic ph-vec" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 256 256" fill="currentColor"><path d="M248,80a28,28,0,1,0-51.12,15.77l-26.79,33L146,73.4a28,28,0,1,0-36.06,0L85.91,128.74l-26.79-33a28,28,0,1,0-26.6,12L47,194.63A16,16,0,0,0,62.78,208H193.22A16,16,0,0,0,209,194.63l14.47-86.85A28,28,0,0,0,248,80ZM128,40a12,12,0,1,1-12,12A12,12,0,0,1,128,40ZM24,80A12,12,0,1,1,36,92,12,12,0,0,1,24,80ZM193.22,192H62.78L48.86,108.52,81.79,149A8,8,0,0,0,88,152a7.83,7.83,0,0,0,1.08-.07,8,8,0,0,0,6.26-4.74l29.3-67.4a27,27,0,0,0,6.72,0l29.3,67.4a8,8,0,0,0,6.26,4.74A7.83,7.83,0,0,0,168,152a8,8,0,0,0,6.21-3l32.93-40.52ZM220,92a12,12,0,1,1,12-12A12,12,0,0,1,220,92Z"/></svg></span><span class="ic-flip-btitle">Dutch Government-in-Exile</span></span><span class="ic-flip-bt">Queen Wilhelmina and the Dutch cabinet set up in London after the Netherlands fell in May 1940. The government retained control of the Dutch colonial empire, including the Dutch East Indies, giving the Allies access to important oil resources until Japan seized those territories in early 1942.</span></span></span></button><button type="button" class="ic-flip"><span class="ic-flip-inner"><span class="ic-flip-face ic-flip-front"><span class="ic-flip-icon"><svg class="ph-ic ph-vec" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 256 256" fill="currentColor"><path d="M208,40H48A16,16,0,0,0,32,56v56c0,52.72,25.52,84.67,46.93,102.19,23.06,18.86,46,25.27,47,25.53a8,8,0,0,0,4.2,0c1-.26,23.91-6.67,47-25.53C198.48,196.67,224,164.72,224,112V56A16,16,0,0,0,208,40Zm0,72c0,37.07-13.66,67.16-40.6,89.42A129.3,129.3,0,0,1,128,223.62a128.25,128.25,0,0,1-38.92-21.81C61.82,179.51,48,149.3,48,112l0-56,160,0Z"/></svg></span><span class="ic-flip-lbl">Yugoslav Government-in-Exile</span></span><span class="ic-flip-face ic-flip-back"><span class="ic-flip-bhead"><span class="ic-flip-bicon"><svg class="ph-ic ph-vec" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 256 256" fill="currentColor"><path d="M208,40H48A16,16,0,0,0,32,56v56c0,52.72,25.52,84.67,46.93,102.19,23.06,18.86,46,25.27,47,25.53a8,8,0,0,0,4.2,0c1-.26,23.91-6.67,47-25.53C198.48,196.67,224,164.72,224,112V56A16,16,0,0,0,208,40Zm0,72c0,37.07-13.66,67.16-40.6,89.42A129.3,129.3,0,0,1,128,223.62a128.25,128.25,0,0,1-38.92-21.81C61.82,179.51,48,149.3,48,112l0-56,160,0Z"/></svg></span><span class="ic-flip-btitle">Yugoslav Government-in-Exile</span></span><span class="ic-flip-bt">King Peter II and his ministers fled to London in April 1941 after the Axis occupied Yugoslavia. The exiled government&#39;s relationship with resistance movements inside Yugoslavia became complicated because two rival groups, the Chetniks and the Partisans, fought each other as well as the occupiers.</span></span></span></button><button type="button" class="ic-flip"><span class="ic-flip-inner"><span class="ic-flip-face ic-flip-front"><span class="ic-flip-icon"><svg class="ph-ic ph-vec" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 256 256" fill="currentColor"><path d="M128,24h0A104,104,0,1,0,232,128,104.12,104.12,0,0,0,128,24Zm88,104a87.61,87.61,0,0,1-3.33,24H174.16a157.44,157.44,0,0,0,0-48h38.51A87.61,87.61,0,0,1,216,128ZM102,168H154a115.11,115.11,0,0,1-26,45A115.27,115.27,0,0,1,102,168Zm-3.9-16a140.84,140.84,0,0,1,0-48h59.88a140.84,140.84,0,0,1,0,48ZM40,128a87.61,87.61,0,0,1,3.33-24H81.84a157.44,157.44,0,0,0,0,48H43.33A87.61,87.61,0,0,1,40,128ZM154,88H102a115.11,115.11,0,0,1,26-45A115.27,115.27,0,0,1,154,88Zm52.33,0H170.71a135.28,135.28,0,0,0-22.3-45.6A88.29,88.29,0,0,1,206.37,88ZM107.59,42.4A135.28,135.28,0,0,0,85.29,88H49.63A88.29,88.29,0,0,1,107.59,42.4ZM49.63,168H85.29a135.28,135.28,0,0,0,22.3,45.6A88.29,88.29,0,0,1,49.63,168Zm98.78,45.6a135.28,135.28,0,0,0,22.3-45.6h35.66A88.29,88.29,0,0,1,148.41,213.6Z"/></svg></span><span class="ic-flip-lbl">Greek Government-in-Exile</span></span><span class="ic-flip-face ic-flip-back"><span class="ic-flip-bhead"><span class="ic-flip-bicon"><svg class="ph-ic ph-vec" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 256 256" fill="currentColor"><path d="M128,24h0A104,104,0,1,0,232,128,104.12,104.12,0,0,0,128,24Zm88,104a87.61,87.61,0,0,1-3.33,24H174.16a157.44,157.44,0,0,0,0-48h38.51A87.61,87.61,0,0,1,216,128ZM102,168H154a115.11,115.11,0,0,1-26,45A115.27,115.27,0,0,1,102,168Zm-3.9-16a140.84,140.84,0,0,1,0-48h59.88a140.84,140.84,0,0,1,0,48ZM40,128a87.61,87.61,0,0,1,3.33-24H81.84a157.44,157.44,0,0,0,0,48H43.33A87.61,87.61,0,0,1,40,128ZM154,88H102a115.11,115.11,0,0,1,26-45A115.27,115.27,0,0,1,154,88Zm52.33,0H170.71a135.28,135.28,0,0,0-22.3-45.6A88.29,88.29,0,0,1,206.37,88ZM107.59,42.4A135.28,135.28,0,0,0,85.29,88H49.63A88.29,88.29,0,0,1,107.59,42.4ZM49.63,168H85.29a135.28,135.28,0,0,0,22.3,45.6A88.29,88.29,0,0,1,49.63,168Zm98.78,45.6a135.28,135.28,0,0,0,22.3-45.6h35.66A88.29,88.29,0,0,1,148.41,213.6Z"/></svg></span><span class="ic-flip-btitle">Greek Government-in-Exile</span></span><span class="ic-flip-bt">After the Axis overran Greece in April 1941, the Greek government moved first to Crete, then to Cairo, and finally to London. It maintained command of Greek naval and air forces that served alongside Allied units in the Mediterranean theater.</span></span></span></button></div></div><div class="ci ci-badge fx-vig" style="background:#15171c"><div class="badge-tex" style="background-image:url('https://historycrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/Paper-1.png');opacity:0.22"></div><div class="badge-ico" style="width:54%;color:#ffffff"><svg class="ph-ic ph-vec" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 256 256" fill="currentColor"><defs><filter id="bdgin23" x="-20%" y="-20%" width="140%" height="140%"><feOffset dx="0" dy="3"/><feGaussianBlur stdDeviation="3" result="ob"/><feComposite operator="out" in="SourceGraphic" in2="ob" result="inv"/><feFlood flood-color="#000000" flood-opacity="0.5" result="cf"/><feComposite operator="in" in="cf" in2="inv" result="sh"/><feComposite operator="over" in="sh" in2="SourceGraphic"/></filter></defs><g filter="url(#bdgin23)"><path d="M216,32H152a8,8,0,0,0-6.34,3.12l-64,83.21L72,108.69a16,16,0,0,0-22.64,0l-12.69,12.7a16,16,0,0,0,0,22.63l20,20-28,28a16,16,0,0,0,0,22.63l12.69,12.68a16,16,0,0,0,22.62,0l28-28,20,20a16,16,0,0,0,22.64,0l12.69-12.7a16,16,0,0,0,0-22.63l-9.64-9.64,83.21-64A8,8,0,0,0,224,104V40A8,8,0,0,0,216,32ZM52.69,216,40,203.32l28-28L80.68,188Zm70.61-8L48,132.71,60.7,120,136,195.31ZM208,100.06l-81.74,62.88L115.32,152l50.34-50.34a8,8,0,0,0-11.32-11.31L104,140.68,93.07,129.74,155.94,48H208Z"/></g></svg></div></div></div><div class="cf" style="background:#fff;border-top:1px solid #f0f0f0"><div class="cf-top"><span class="cf-src" style="color:#999" title="Source: Smithsonian Institution">Source: Smithsonian Institution</span><span class="cf-copy" style="color:#999">© factsthatcrunch.com</span><a class="cf-more" style="color:#B0851E" href="https://factsthatcrunch.com/world-war-ii-facts/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">More World War II Facts →</a></div></div></div></div>
</div>

<!--ftc-embed-end-->




<div style="height:50px" aria-hidden="true" class="wp-block-spacer"></div>



<h2 id="allied-powers-of-world-war-ii-how-did-it-form" class="wp-block-heading">ALLIED POWERS OF WORLD WAR II &#8211; HOW DID IT FORM?</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Another major step came after the <a href="https://historycrunch.com/attack-on-pearl-harbor/" data-type="post" data-id="3535">Japanese attack on Pearl Harbor</a> on December 7th of 1941. For instance, just weeks later on January 1st, 1942, representatives of 26 nations signed the &#8216;Declaration by United Nations&#8217; in Washington, D.C.. This agreement committed them to continue fighting the Axis Powers and to avoid making a separate peace. This was very significant because it gave the Allied war effort a clearer sense of unity and purpose. In fact, the term &#8216;United Nations&#8217; was first used during World War II to describe the countries fighting together against the Axis Powers.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The Allied alliance developed over time as World War II grew larger and more destructive. In the early stage of the conflict, the main powers resisting Nazi Germany were Britain and France, but this changed as Adolf Hitler’s conquests spread across Europe and as other major powers were drawn into the war. One important step in the formation of the Allied partnership came in August of 1941, when Franklin D. Roosevelt and Winston Churchill issued the Atlantic Charter, which was released on August 14th, 1941. This statement described broad Allied goals, including the defeat of Nazi tyranny, freedom of the seas and the right of people to choose their own government.</p><!--ftc-embed-start-->

<div id="ftc1zhhuf" class="ftc-embed ftc-lean" data-ftcv="773" style="max-width:920px;margin:0 auto">
<div class="ftc-card-scope" data-type="lavaquote" data-category="Lava" data-topic="World War II" data-subtopic="Allied Leaders" data-title="" data-source="Franklin D. Roosevelt Presidential Library and Museum"><div class="card img-none cc-vig box-lift bs-raised bsh-rounded fx-lift nb-spiral nb-tape nb-torn nb-doodle nb-hlhand nb-dogear nb-overshoot fx-sheen expr-round lc-title fbs-gloss fwm" style="--cc-bg:#fbf1f5;--hl:#ffd54f;--ul:#36e0c8;--accent:#C9366F;--acc-glow:rgba(201,54,111,0.3);--acc-glow2:rgba(201,54,111,0.186);--acc-glow-blur:26px;--flip-h:100px;--lead-scale:1;--rp-arrow-size:30px;--rp-arrow-gap:12px;--tp-arrow-size:34px;--kt-termw:150px;--ps-gap:8px;--ven-size:100%;--def-pad:7px;--mtl-yr-w:98px;--exp-fill:rgba(201,54,111,0.1);--g3d:1.3;--flip-bg:#3a3a3a;--flip-light:#e3e3e3;--flip-cols:2;--flip-icon-size:42px;--flip-fsize:15px;--flip-weight:600;--tex-op:0.19;--vs-size:40px;--vs-pad:10px;--bar-h:18px;--ce-arrow:60px;--ce-arrow-pad:8px;--img-fr:0.72fr;--imgcolw:40%;--tex-url:url('https://historycrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/Paper-9-scaled.png');--tex-tf:rotate(180deg) scaleX(-1);--vig-img-blur:40px;--vig-img-op:0.45;--vig-cc-blur:20px;--vig-cc-op:0.4;--fade-dur:4s;box-shadow:3px 6px 22px rgba(0,0,0,0.24),0 1px 4px rgba(0,0,0,0.05)"><div class="cb"><div class="ic-bignum ic-slv" data-itype="sleeklava"><span class="ic-lava-blob ic-lb1" style="top:auto;bottom:-90px;--lvo:1.12;--lvs:1.08;--lvd:1.00"></span><span class="ic-lava-blob ic-lb2" style="bottom:auto;top:-120px;--lvo:1.12;--lvs:1.08;--lvd:1.00"></span><span class="ic-lava-blob ic-lb3" style="bottom:auto;top:26%;--lvo:1.12;--lvs:1.08;--lvd:1.00"></span><div class="ic-slv-in"><div class="ic-slv-big ic-lvq-big ic-slv-big-sm">“the final destruction of the Nazi tyranny”</div><div class="ic-slv-lab">Franklin D. Roosevelt and Winston Churchill, Placentia Bay, Newfoundland, August 14th, 1941</div><div class="ic-slv-ctx">The Atlantic Charter listed <span class="hl-underline">eight shared goals</span> for the world after the war, including self-government for all peoples and freer trade. The United States had not yet entered the fighting, so this open call to end Nazi rule showed the world where America stood.</div></div><div class="ic-sleek-footgrad"></div><div class="ic-sleek-foot"><span class="ic-sleek-fsrc">Source: Franklin D. Roosevelt Presidential Library and Museum</span><span class="ic-sleek-fcopy">© factsthatcrunch.com</span><a class="ic-sleek-logo2" href="https://factsthatcrunch.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img decoding="async" src="https://factsthatcrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/facts-that-crunch-logo.png" alt="Facts that Crunch"></a></div></div></div></div></div>
</div>

<!--ftc-embed-end-->




<div style="height:50px" aria-hidden="true" class="wp-block-spacer"></div>



<h2 id="allied-powers-of-wordl-war-ii-issues" class="wp-block-heading">ALLIED POWERS OF WORDL WAR II &#8211; ISSUES</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Although the Allied Powers worked together effectively enough to win the war, the alliance was never free of tension, and many of its members distrusted one another from the very beginning. For instance, Winston Churchill worried deeply about Soviet expansion into Eastern Europe, while Joseph Stalin believed that Britain and the United States had waited too long to open a second major front in western Europe. Franklin D. Roosevelt often tried to keep the alliance united, but even he understood that the wartime partnership included serious disagreements about ideology, military strategy and the future of the world after the war.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">These tensions became especially important as Allied victory approached in 1945, because the leaders had to decide what would happen to Germany, Poland, and the rest of Europe once the fighting ended. In many ways, the disagreements that appeared during the final years of the war helped create the mistrust that would soon lead to the <a href="https://historycrunch.com/cold-war/" data-type="page" data-id="8547">Cold War</a> between the United States and the Soviet Union. Therefore, the Allied alliance was both a great military success and a temporary partnership that contained the seeds of future conflict. As such, historians often consider the Yalta Conference and Potsdam Conference to be important <a href="https://historycrunch.com/causes-of-the-cold-war/" data-type="post" data-id="6267">causes of the Cold War</a>.</p><!--ftc-embed-start-->

<div id="ftc93x2xd" class="ftc-embed ftc-lean" data-ftcv="773" style="max-width:920px;margin:0 auto">
<div class="ftc-card-scope" data-type="ntce" data-category="Student Notes" data-topic="World War II" data-subtopic="Start of the Cold War" data-title="How Allied Wartime Distrust Led to the Cold War" data-source="National Archives and Records Administration"><div class="card nt-full nt-flair nt-k-ce nt-ink-navy nt-lab-hl nt-redline" data-itype="ntce" style="--ink:#26324d"><div class="ch"><div class="ch-bar"><div class="hl"><span class="ctt">World War II</span><span class="cty" style="color:#0E7490">Notebook Cause and Effect</span></div><a class="logo-link" href="https://factsthatcrunch.com" target="_blank" style="transform:translate(0px,5px);" rel="noopener"><img decoding="async" style="height:36px" src="https://factsthatcrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/facts-that-crunch-logo.png" alt="Facts that Crunch" onerror="this.style.display='none';this.nextElementSibling.style.display='flex'"><span class="logo-fb"><span class="lb"><span>Facts</span></span><span class="lt">that</span><span class="lb"><span>Crunch</span></span></span></a></div><div class="ch-line"><div class="ch-line-fill" style="background:#0E7490"></div></div></div><div class="cb"><div class="cc" style="background:var(--cc-bg,#fff)"><div class="ic-wt"><div class="ic-wt-window"><span class="ic-wt-tape ic-wt-tape-l"></span><span class="ic-wt-fx ic-wt-dogear"></span><span class="ic-ntd-stamp"><svg class="ph-ic ph-vec" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 256 256" fill="currentColor"><path d="M221.66,133.66l-72,72a8,8,0,0,1-11.32-11.32L196.69,136H40a8,8,0,0,1,0-16H196.69L138.34,61.66a8,8,0,0,1,11.32-11.32l72,72A8,8,0,0,1,221.66,133.66Z"/></svg></span><span class="ic-nt-rail" aria-hidden="true"><span class="ic-nt-note ic-nt-note1 ic-pfa" aria-hidden="true">Old allies, new enemies</span><span class="ic-nt-key ic-pfa" aria-hidden="true"><b>★</b> Molotov-Ribbentrop Pact</span><span class="ic-nt-note ic-nt-note2 ic-pfa" aria-hidden="true">Mistrust outlasted the war</span><span class="ic-nt-sticky ic-pfb" aria-hidden="true">cause and effect!</span></span><div class="ic-ntd"><div class="ic-ntd-term"><span class="nt-w" style="transform:rotate(0deg) translateY(0px);opacity:0.96">How</span> <span class="nt-w" style="transform:rotate(1.5deg) translateY(-0.5px);opacity:0.93">Allied</span> <span class="nt-w" style="transform:rotate(-2deg) translateY(0px);opacity:0.99">Wartime</span> <span class="nt-w" style="transform:rotate(1deg) translateY(-0.5px);opacity:0.97">Distrust</span> <span class="nt-w" style="transform:rotate(0.5deg) translateY(1px);opacity:0.91">Led</span> <span class="nt-w" style="transform:rotate(1.5deg) translateY(0px);opacity:0.91">to</span> <span class="nt-w" style="transform:rotate(0.5deg) translateY(0px);opacity:0.92">the</span> <span class="nt-w" style="transform:rotate(1.5deg) translateY(-0.5px);opacity:0.96">Cold</span> <span class="nt-w" style="transform:rotate(0deg) translateY(-0.5px);opacity:0.88">War</span></div><div class="ic-ce2"><div class="ic-ce2-col"><div class="ic-ce2-hd">Causes</div><div class="ic-ce2-item">The Soviet Union signed the <span class="hl-underline">Molotov-Ribbentrop Pact</span> with Nazi Germany in August 1939, alarming Britain and France before the war even began.</div><div class="ic-ce2-item">Western Allies delayed opening a major second front in Western Europe until June 1944, which Stalin believed was deliberate.</div><div class="ic-ce2-item">At the <span class="hl-underline">Yalta Conference</span> in February 1945, disagreements over Poland&#39;s postwar government showed deep divisions between the Soviet Union and the Western powers.</div><div class="ic-ce2-item">The United States shared atomic bomb research with Britain but kept the <span class="hl-underline">Manhattan Project</span> secret from the Soviet Union throughout the war.</div></div><div class="ic-ce2-mid"><span class="ic-ce2-arr" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 34 18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M2 9.5C10 8.5 19 8.5 30 9"/><path d="M30 9l-7-4.6M30 9l-6.6 5"/></svg></span><div class="ic-ce2-ev">Allied distrust fuels Cold War</div><span class="ic-ce2-arr" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 34 18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M2 9.5C10 8.5 19 8.5 30 9"/><path d="M30 9l-7-4.6M30 9l-6.6 5"/></svg></span></div><div class="ic-ce2-col"><div class="ic-ce2-hd">Effects</div><div class="ic-ce2-item">After the war, the Soviet Union installed <span class="hl-underline">communist governments</span> across Eastern Europe rather than allowing free elections.</div><div class="ic-ce2-item">The United States and Britain viewed Soviet expansion as a threat, leading to the <span class="hl-underline">Truman Doctrine</span> in March 1947.</div><div class="ic-ce2-item">Germany itself was divided into <span class="hl-underline">occupation zones</span> that hardened into separate states, reflecting the split between former allies.</div><div class="ic-ce2-item">By 1949, both sides had formed rival military alliances, with the <span class="hl-underline">NATO</span> treaty binding Western nations against Soviet power.</div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="cf" style="background:#fff;border-top:1px solid #f0f0f0"><div class="cf-top"><span class="cf-src" style="color:#999" title="Source: National Archives and Records Administration">Source: National Archives and Records Administration</span><span class="cf-copy" style="color:#999">© factsthatcrunch.com</span><a class="cf-more" style="color:#0E7490" href="https://factsthatcrunch.com/world-war-ii-facts/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">More World War II Facts →</a></div></div></div></div>
</div>

<!--ftc-embed-end-->




<div style="height:50px" aria-hidden="true" class="wp-block-spacer"></div>



<h2 id="allied-powers-of-world-war-ii-victory" class="wp-block-heading">ALLIED POWERS OF WORLD WAR II &#8211; VICTORY</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">By 1945, the Allied Powers had defeated <a href="https://historycrunch.com/fascism-in-italy/" data-type="post" data-id="8582">fascist Italy</a>, Nazi Germany and Imperial Japan. Although, it should be noted that the fighting in Europe ended before the war in Asia and the Pacific. In Europe, Allied armies pushed toward Germany from both east and west, while German cities (such as <a href="https://historycrunch.com/bombing-of-dresden-in-world-war-ii/" data-type="post" data-id="3545">Dresden</a>) were heavily bombed and Hitler’s military position collapsed. Adolf Hitler died by suicide in Berlin, Germany on April 30th, 1945, and Nazi Germany officially surrendered on May 8th, 1945, which became known as V-E Day, or Victory in Europe Day.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">However, the war continued against Imperial Japan for several more months. Japan was already under enormous pressure from American bombing, naval blockade, and battlefield defeat, but the final stage of the war was sealed with the American <a href="https://historycrunch.com/atomic-bombing-of-japan/" data-type="post" data-id="3069">atomic bombings of Hiroshima</a> on August 6th, 1945, and Nagasaki on August 9th, 1945. As a result of these two bombings, Japan formally surrendered on September 2nd, 1945, which was the formal end of the fighting in World War II. The Allied Powers had proven victorious over the Axis Powers.</p><!--ftc-embed-start-->

<div id="ftcxnkz5k" class="ftc-embed ftc-lean" data-ftcv="773" style="max-width:920px;margin:0 auto">
<div class="ftc-card-scope" data-type="timeline" data-category="Information" data-topic="World War II" data-subtopic="How Did World War II End?" data-title="" data-source="Library of Congress"><div class="card img-none cc-vig box-lift bs-raised bsh-rounded fx-lift nb-spiral nb-tape nb-torn nb-doodle nb-hlhand nb-dogear nb-overshoot fx-sheen expr-round lc-title fbs-gloss fwm" style="--cc-bg:#eef7f5;--hl:#ffd54f;--ul:#36e0c8;--accent:#0E8A6F;--acc-glow:rgba(14,138,111,0.3);--acc-glow2:rgba(14,138,111,0.186);--acc-glow-blur:26px;--flip-h:100px;--lead-scale:1;--rp-arrow-size:30px;--rp-arrow-gap:12px;--tp-arrow-size:34px;--kt-termw:150px;--ps-gap:8px;--ven-size:100%;--def-pad:7px;--mtl-yr-w:98px;--exp-fill:rgba(14,138,111,0.1);--g3d:1.3;--flip-bg:#3a3a3a;--flip-light:#e3e3e3;--flip-cols:2;--flip-icon-size:42px;--flip-fsize:15px;--flip-weight:600;--tex-op:0.19;--vs-size:40px;--vs-pad:10px;--bar-h:18px;--ce-arrow:60px;--ce-arrow-pad:8px;--img-fr:0.72fr;--imgcolw:40%;--tex-url:url('https://historycrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/Paper-8-scaled.png');--tex-tf:none;--vig-img-blur:40px;--vig-img-op:0.45;--vig-cc-blur:20px;--vig-cc-op:0.4;--fade-dur:4s;box-shadow:3px 6px 22px rgba(0,0,0,0.24),0 1px 4px rgba(0,0,0,0.05)"><div class="ch"><div class="ch-bar"><div class="hl"><span class="ctt">World War II</span><span class="cty" style="color:#0E8A6F">Mini Timeline</span></div><a class="logo-link" href="https://factsthatcrunch.com" target="_blank" style="transform:translate(0px,5px);" rel="noopener"><img decoding="async" style="height:36px" src="https://factsthatcrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/facts-that-crunch-logo.png" alt="Facts that Crunch" onerror="this.style.display='none';this.nextElementSibling.style.display='flex'"><span class="logo-fb"><span class="lb"><span>Facts</span></span><span class="lt">that</span><span class="lb"><span>Crunch</span></span></span></a></div><div class="ch-line"><div class="ch-line-fill" style="background:#0E8A6F"></div></div></div><div class="tl-split"><div class="tl-left"><div class="mtl-intro"><div class="split-line" style="font-family:'Barlow Condensed',sans-serif;font-size:21px;font-weight:700;color:#0E8A6F;line-height:1.25"><span class="split-line-inner">After Adolf Hitler died on April 30th, 1945, the war in Europe moved quickly toward a close. Allied forces pressed into Germany from the west and east at the same time. The surrender of Germany opened the way for the final push against Japan.</span></div></div><div class="mtl"><div class="mtl-item"><div class="mtl-yr" style="background:#085041">April 30th, 1945</div><div class="mtl-con"><div class="mtl-dot" style="background:#085041;box-shadow:0 0 0 2px #085041"></div><div class="mtl-seg"></div></div><div class="mtl-ev"><strong>Hitler Dies in Berlin</strong>Hitler shot himself in his underground bunker as Soviet troops fought inside the city.</div></div><div class="mtl-item"><div class="mtl-yr" style="background:#1D9E75">May 2nd, 1945</div><div class="mtl-con"><div class="mtl-dot" style="background:#1D9E75;box-shadow:0 0 0 2px #1D9E75"></div><div class="mtl-seg"></div></div><div class="mtl-ev"><strong>Berlin Falls</strong>German forces in Berlin surrendered to Soviet commanders, ending the battle for the German capital.</div></div><div class="mtl-item"><div class="mtl-yr" style="background:#f59e0b">May 7th, 1945</div><div class="mtl-con"><div class="mtl-dot" style="background:#f59e0b;box-shadow:0 0 0 2px #f59e0b"></div><div class="mtl-seg"></div></div><div class="mtl-ev"><strong>Germany Signs Surrender</strong>German military leaders signed an unconditional surrender at Allied headquarters in Reims, France.</div></div><div class="mtl-item"><div class="mtl-yr" style="background:#cc3333">May 8th, 1945</div><div class="mtl-con"><div class="mtl-dot" style="background:#cc3333;box-shadow:0 0 0 2px #cc3333"></div><div class="mtl-seg"></div></div><div class="mtl-ev"><strong>V-E Day Declared</strong>The Allies named May 8th, 1945 Victory in Europe Day, marking the official end of the war in Europe.</div></div><div class="mtl-item"><div class="mtl-yr" style="background:#185FA5">June 21st, 1945</div><div class="mtl-con"><div class="mtl-dot" style="background:#185FA5;box-shadow:0 0 0 2px #185FA5"></div></div><div class="mtl-ev"><strong>Battle of Okinawa Ends</strong>After nearly three months of fighting, the Allies secured Okinawa, giving them a base within 340 miles (550 kilometers) of the Japanese mainland.</div></div></div></div><div class="tl-right"><span class="tl-ico tl-ico1" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="ph-ic ph-vec" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 256 256" fill="currentColor"><path d="M92,104a28,28,0,1,0,28,28A28,28,0,0,0,92,104Zm0,40a12,12,0,1,1,12-12A12,12,0,0,1,92,144Zm72-40a28,28,0,1,0,28,28A28,28,0,0,0,164,104Zm0,40a12,12,0,1,1,12-12A12,12,0,0,1,164,144ZM128,16C70.65,16,24,60.86,24,116c0,34.1,18.27,66,48,84.28V216a16,16,0,0,0,16,16h80a16,16,0,0,0,16-16V200.28C213.73,182,232,150.1,232,116,232,60.86,185.35,16,128,16Zm44.12,172.69a8,8,0,0,0-4.12,7V216H152V192a8,8,0,0,0-16,0v24H120V192a8,8,0,0,0-16,0v24H88V195.69a8,8,0,0,0-4.12-7C56.81,173.69,40,145.84,40,116c0-46.32,39.48-84,88-84s88,37.68,88,84C216,145.83,199.19,173.69,172.12,188.69Z"/></svg></span><div class="tl-stat"><div class="tl-stat-num">1 million+</div><div class="tl-stat-lbl">Allied troops involved in the Okinawa campaign</div></div><div class="tl-stat"><div class="tl-stat-num">8 days</div><div class="tl-stat-lbl">Time between Germany&#39;s surrender signing and V-E Day celebrations</div></div><div class="tl-stat"><div class="tl-stat-num">23 days</div><div class="tl-stat-lbl">Length of the Battle of Berlin, from April 16th to May 8th, 1945</div></div><span class="tl-ico tl-ico2" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="ph-ic ph-vec" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 256 256" fill="currentColor"><path d="M254.3,107.91,228.78,56.85a16,16,0,0,0-21.47-7.15L182.44,62.13,130.05,48.27a8.14,8.14,0,0,0-4.1,0L73.56,62.13,48.69,49.7a16,16,0,0,0-21.47,7.15L1.7,107.9a16,16,0,0,0,7.15,21.47l27,13.51,55.49,39.63a8.06,8.06,0,0,0,2.71,1.25l64,16a8,8,0,0,0,7.6-2.1l55.07-55.08,26.42-13.21a16,16,0,0,0,7.15-21.46Zm-54.89,33.37L165,113.72a8,8,0,0,0-10.68.61C136.51,132.27,116.66,130,104,122L147.24,80h31.81l27.21,54.41ZM41.53,64,62,74.22,36.43,125.27,16,115.06Zm116,119.13L99.42,168.61l-49.2-35.14,28-56L128,64.28l9.8,2.59-45,43.68-.08.09a16,16,0,0,0,2.72,24.81c20.56,13.13,45.37,11,64.91-5L188,152.66Zm62-57.87-25.52-51L214.47,64,240,115.06Zm-87.75,92.67a8,8,0,0,1-7.75,6.06,8.13,8.13,0,0,1-1.95-.24L80.41,213.33a7.89,7.89,0,0,1-2.71-1.25L51.35,193.26a8,8,0,0,1,9.3-13l25.11,17.94L126,208.24A8,8,0,0,1,131.82,217.94Z"/></svg></span></div></div><div class="cf" style="background:#fff;border-top:1px solid #f0f0f0"><div class="cf-top"><span class="cf-src" style="color:#999" title="Source: Library of Congress">Source: Library of Congress</span><span class="cf-copy" style="color:#999">© factsthatcrunch.com</span><a class="cf-more" style="color:#0E8A6F" href="https://factsthatcrunch.com/world-war-ii-facts/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">More World War II Facts →</a></div></div></div></div>
</div>

<!--ftc-embed-end-->




<div style="height:50px" aria-hidden="true" class="wp-block-spacer"></div>



<h2 id="allied-powers-of-world-war-ii-significance" class="wp-block-heading">ALLIED POWERS OF WORLD WAR II &#8211; SIGNIFICANCE</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The Allied Powers were significant because they defeated the Axis Powers and prevented Nazi Germany, fascist Italy, and Imperial Japan from gaining lasting control over large parts of Europe, Africa, Asia and the Pacific. Their victory brought an end to some of the most aggressive and destructive regimes in modern history, and it also made possible the liberation of many countries that had been conquered and occupied during the war. As well, the Allied Powers helped put an end to terrible atrocities such as the <a href="https://historycrunch.com/holocaust/" data-type="page" data-id="8953">Holocaust</a>.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The Allied Powers are also historically important because they show how countries with very different governments, leaders, and beliefs can cooperate when facing a common enemy. Britain, the United States, the Soviet Union, China, and Free France did not agree on everything, and in some cases they disagreed very strongly, but they were still able to combine their military strength, industrial production, resources, and leadership in a way that changed world history.</p><!--ftc-embed-start-->

<div id="ftcp3k3y5" class="ftc-embed ftc-lean" data-ftcv="773" style="max-width:920px;margin:0 auto">
<div class="ftc-card-scope" data-type="mc" data-category="Quiz" data-topic="World War II" data-subtopic="Significance of World War II" data-title="" data-source="US National Archives"><div class="card img-none cc-vig box-lift bs-raised bsh-rounded acc-glow fx-lift nb-spiral nb-tape nb-torn nb-doodle nb-hlhand nb-dogear nb-overshoot fx-sheen expr-round lc-title fbs-gloss fwm" style="--cc-bg:#eff4f9;--hl:#ffd54f;--ul:#36e0c8;--accent:#185FA5;--acc-glow:rgba(24,95,165,0.3);--acc-glow2:rgba(24,95,165,0.186);--acc-glow-blur:26px;--flip-h:100px;--lead-scale:1;--rp-arrow-size:30px;--rp-arrow-gap:12px;--tp-arrow-size:34px;--kt-termw:150px;--ps-gap:8px;--ven-size:100%;--def-pad:7px;--mtl-yr-w:98px;--exp-fill:rgba(24,95,165,0.1);--g3d:1.3;--flip-bg:#3a3a3a;--flip-light:#e3e3e3;--flip-cols:2;--flip-icon-size:42px;--flip-fsize:15px;--flip-weight:600;--tex-op:0.19;--vs-size:40px;--vs-pad:10px;--bar-h:18px;--ce-arrow:60px;--ce-arrow-pad:8px;--img-fr:0.72fr;--imgcolw:40%;--tex-url:url('https://historycrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/Concrete-6-scaled.png');--tex-tf:scaleY(-1);--vig-img-blur:40px;--vig-img-op:0.45;--vig-cc-blur:20px;--vig-cc-op:0.4;--fade-dur:4s;box-shadow:3px 6px 22px rgba(0,0,0,0.24),0 1px 4px rgba(0,0,0,0.05)"><div class="ch"><div class="ch-bar"><div class="hl"><span class="ctt">World War II</span><span class="cty" style="color:#185FA5">Multiple Choice</span><span class="ix-badge">Interactive</span></div><a class="logo-link" href="https://factsthatcrunch.com" target="_blank" style="transform:translate(0px,5px);" rel="noopener"><img decoding="async" style="height:36px" src="https://factsthatcrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/facts-that-crunch-logo.png" alt="Facts that Crunch" onerror="this.style.display='none';this.nextElementSibling.style.display='flex'"><span class="logo-fb"><span class="lb"><span>Facts</span></span><span class="lt">that</span><span class="lb"><span>Crunch</span></span></span></a></div><div class="ch-line"><div class="ch-line-fill" style="background:#185FA5"></div></div></div><div class="cb"><div class="cc" style="background:var(--cc-bg,#fff)"><p class="ic-q">What did <span class="hl-phrase">Allied cooperation in World War II</span> show about governments working together across major political differences?</p><div style="font-size:12px;font-style:italic;color:#9a9a9a;margin-bottom:8px">Tap an option.</div><div class="ic-opts" data-itype="quiz"><button type="button" class="ic-opt" data-correct="1"><span class="ic-opt-t">Democracies and a communist state could set aside political differences to fight a shared enemy</span></button><button type="button" class="ic-opt"><span class="ic-opt-t">Allied nations agreed to adopt the same political system after the war ended</span></button><button type="button" class="ic-opt"><span class="ic-opt-t">The Allied governments merged their armies under a single shared command for the whole war</span></button><button type="button" class="ic-opt"><span class="ic-opt-t">Cooperation required all Allied nations to sign a formal military alliance before fighting began</span></button></div><div class="ic-explain"><span class="ic-explain-l">Explanation</span>The United States and Britain were capitalist democracies while the Soviet Union was a communist state. Despite deep political differences, all three worked together through agreements such as the <span class="hl-phrase">Tehran Conference of 1943</span>, sharing resources and coordinating strategy against Germany and Japan. They never adopted the same system or merged their forces into one command.</div></div><div class="ci ci-badge fx-vig" style="background:#15171c"><div class="badge-tex" style="background-image:url('https://historycrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/Paper-1.png');opacity:0.22"></div><div class="badge-ico" style="width:54%;color:#ffffff"><svg class="ph-ic ph-vec" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 256 256" fill="currentColor"><defs><filter id="bdgin25" x="-20%" y="-20%" width="140%" height="140%"><feOffset dx="0" dy="3"/><feGaussianBlur stdDeviation="3" result="ob"/><feComposite operator="out" in="SourceGraphic" in2="ob" result="inv"/><feFlood flood-color="#000000" flood-opacity="0.5" result="cf"/><feComposite operator="in" in="cf" in2="inv" result="sh"/><feComposite operator="over" in="sh" in2="SourceGraphic"/></filter></defs><g filter="url(#bdgin25)"><path d="M216,32H152a8,8,0,0,0-6.34,3.12l-64,83.21L72,108.69a16,16,0,0,0-22.64,0l-12.69,12.7a16,16,0,0,0,0,22.63l20,20-28,28a16,16,0,0,0,0,22.63l12.69,12.68a16,16,0,0,0,22.62,0l28-28,20,20a16,16,0,0,0,22.64,0l12.69-12.7a16,16,0,0,0,0-22.63l-9.64-9.64,83.21-64A8,8,0,0,0,224,104V40A8,8,0,0,0,216,32ZM52.69,216,40,203.32l28-28L80.68,188Zm70.61-8L48,132.71,60.7,120,136,195.31ZM208,100.06l-81.74,62.88L115.32,152l50.34-50.34a8,8,0,0,0-11.32-11.31L104,140.68,93.07,129.74,155.94,48H208Z"/></g></svg></div></div></div><div class="cf" style="background:#fff;border-top:1px solid #f0f0f0"><div class="cf-top"><span class="cf-src" style="color:#999" title="Source: US National Archives">Source: US National Archives</span><span class="cf-copy" style="color:#999">© factsthatcrunch.com</span><a class="cf-more" style="color:#185FA5" href="https://factsthatcrunch.com/world-war-ii-facts/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">More World War II Facts →</a></div></div></div></div>
</div>

